\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Reflection positivity on a finite time lattice}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Where could time evolution come from when a law describes all lattice
sites at once? Choose one direction to test as Euclidean time. Reflection
positivity asks whether observables on one half of the lattice have a
positive semidefinite pairing with their reflected partners. Quotienting
the vectors of zero length and completing gives a Hilbert space.
Reflection positivity is stronger than invariance of the probability
law under reflection. The two-site
Gaussian makes the sign of a crossing interaction visible.}

\p{The scalar result below uses a finite nearest-neighbor torus,
reflection-invariant nonnegative weights and positive mass. Its
proof first treats a bounded field range, then removes that cutoff.
Site reflections retain shared boundary coordinates; link reflections
split the sites into disjoint halves. Neither choice makes a time
translation an operator on the resulting quotient without further
assumptions.}

\p{A step of evolution therefore needs more than this positive pairing.
The later [Gaussian transfer operator](fdip-007S) uses a nearest-neighbor
kernel with positive weight and mass and a specified reference measure;
the [gauge transfer construction](fdip-007Y) specifies a positive
character kernel and gauge projection. Their spectral hypotheses and
normalizations permit a Hamiltonian logarithm. These are Euclidean
transfer constructions, not an automatic normalized real-time
probability evolution.}

